07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "No time-respecting cascade exists from 1357 to 1439 under the 600-unit relay constraint. Direct test (step_01) returns reached=false, and path enumeration (step_09) yields zero routes. This is not an artifact of an overly tight delay budget: relaxing max_wait entirely (step_03) still fails to reach 1439, expanding the frontier only from 73 to 226 of 1914 active nodes. So the barrier is structural-temporal, not a matter of waiting longer. The reverse direction also fails (step_06). Confounders ruled out: (a) Volume/popularity \u2014 both nodes are high-degree hubs (69 and 68 events, step_05) in a busy window of 5931 events (step_02), and both stay active across the whole span in a U-shaped pattern with overlapping bins (step_13, step_12 jaccard 0.10); ample activity and co-presence nonetheless produce no carrier chain. (b) Static connectivity \u2014 there is no direct edge in either direction (step_11), and the two neighbourhoods are entirely disjoint: 1357's 68 out-neighbours (step_08) share not one member with 1439's 68 contacts (step_07). 1439's 1-hop subgraph (step_10) is an almost pure star, its only non-star tie being 192-1998, so it has essentially no bridging structure to ingest a cascade. (c) Endpoint-specific failure \u2014 1357 cannot even reach 1439's feeder nodes 1105 or 651 (step_14, step_15), each stopping at the same 73-node frontier, which barely exceeds 1357's own 68 direct partners. The cascade therefore dies at the first hop rather than being blocked only at the final step. Verdict: absent."}, "raw_final_response": "All evidence converges. Final conclusion:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_ada3f151\",\n  